  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1012 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nTonight through Monday...\n\nTemperatures continued to plunge this evening, as northwesterly \nwinds gusted behind a passing cold front. By 10 PM, temperatures\nhad fallen into the lower 40s across the region, with lows \nexpected to reach the mid 20s to mid 30s by early Sunday \nmorning. Showers and low clouds will continue to progress \nsoutheastward behind the front, exiting the forecast area by 3 \nAM.\n\nHigh pressure will filter in behind the front Sunday through mid \nweek. Expect clearing skies on Sunday, with patchy clouds on Monday. \nCooler air will continue to pour into the region. Highs on Sunday \nwill top out in the 40s and only reach the 30s to low 40s Monday. \nOvernight lows will dip into the teens and 20s. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday\n\nCool temperatures linger through Tuesday and into Wednesday, with \nhighs remaining in the lower 40s and below. An upper low will \ndip into the the central CONUS by midweek. While there remains \nseveral differences in timing and location of the low amongst\nthe different ensemble members, overall trends indicate that a \nlack of deep layer moisture will likely prevent us from seeing \nany appreciable precipitation. \n\nThursday through Saturday:\n\nThe H5 closed low will eject southeast away from the forecast area \nThanksgiving morning. Sfc high pressure moves in behind the \ndeparting feature resulting in continued quiet weather and highs in \nthe 40s. \n\nAlthough there is some model spread in the placement and track of \nthe departing H5 low, northwesterly flow returns into the area by \nFriday as an H5 ridge to our west amplifies some. A compact wave \nwill eject toward the area as well on Friday, while a sfc low spins \nup and stays primarily across our north and east. A weak front from \nthe sfc low will swing through the area, but at this time expect dry \nconditions to prevail as little moisture will be available for the \nfeature to work with. PoPs remain less than 12% at this time across \nour area. Quiet conditions continue into Saturday as a channel of \nsfc high pressure moves across the Northern Plains.\n\nExpanding 1000-500mb thicknesses across the region will result in a \nwarming temperature trend, particularly on Friday and Saturday with \nhighs reaching the upper 40s to low 50s.",
